$CATEGORY: LIPPS/Epidemiology/Week 09/Adjustment

::EPI-W9-044::Which variable-selection rule is safest? {
=Choose adjustment variables using causal and subject-matter reasoning, not p-values alone#Correct.
~Adjust for every measured variable#Review causal structure rather than statistical association alone.
~Adjust only variables with p below 0.05#Review causal structure rather than statistical association alone.
~Never adjust observational studies#Review causal structure rather than statistical association alone.
}

::EPI-W9-045::What is wrong with the statement 'we adjusted for everything, therefore confounding is gone'? {
=Some variables may be mediators or colliders, and unmeasured or residual confounding can remain#Correct.
~More variables always guarantee validity#Adjustment quality depends on causal structure and measurement, not the number of covariates.
~Confounding exists only in small samples#Adjustment quality depends on causal structure and measurement, not the number of covariates.
~Adjustment makes measurement error impossible#Adjustment quality depends on causal structure and measurement, not the number of covariates.
}

